Data Structures and Algorithms

Data structures are ways to store and organize data in a computer. An algorithm is a set of steps used to solve a problem. They work together to help programs manage data.

Linear Data Structure

A linear data structure stores data in order, one after another. Examples are arrays, linked lists, stacks, and queues.

One-Dimensional Array

A one-dimensional array stores values in one line. Each value has an index that usually starts at 0.

Example:

[10, 20, 30, 40, 50]
Big O Notation

Big O Notation shows how the time needed by an algorithm changes when the amount of data gets bigger.

Examples:

O(1) – stays almost the same
O(log n) – grows slowly
O(n) – grows with the data
O(n²) – grows quickly
Basic Searching Algorithms

Searching algorithms are used to find an item in a group of data.

Linear Search

Linear Search checks each item one by one until it finds the item. It can work with unsorted data and has a time complexity of O(n).

Binary Search

Binary Search checks the middle item and removes half of the search area each time. The data must be sorted. It has a time complexity of O(log n).

